It depends on the game.  In most dungeons, there's an
average of something like 1.5 not-so-portable containers
(chests, large boxes) per level, but I've had games in
which I had to descend five or six levels to find one.

> (although the tourist also has a way to open locked
> containers, which helps).

If I don't have an unlocking device, I usually pet-test
every single junk edged weapon I encounter until
I find one that's not cursed, and use that to #force
chest locks on the early levels.  If the first one I
find is unnecessarily heavy, I'll trade down to a
dagger once I find one, or a knife in a pinch.

Leaving stuff in a non-locked chest is almost always
good enough in the early game, and if you make it
to the middle game you're going to transfer it all
(well, any of it you care about) to a stash on a
lower level anyway, preferably a level with a co-
aligned altar if you can find such a thing.

I think I've only played one game so far where I
got past level 2 without finding any non-cursed
edged weapons I could afford to lose.  It was
bizarre:  all the monsters were animals, so
there were, like, no weapons in the dungeon,
until I got into the mines and started seeing
Gnomes.

Of course, once you have a knife #named
LockBane, you start seeing daggers laying
around everyplace, but that's just the RNG's
usual tricks -- just like once you finally after
twelve levels of searching find a skeleton
key, you then see four more keys within the
next two levels.


